          "text": "{\"relation\":\"MATCH\",\"consensus_summary\":\"“Faithful Witness” is primarily a Christological title for Jesus as the perfectly reliable revealer and testifier of God’s truth, with possible Davidic, martyrological, and lunar-covenantal associations.\",\"book_summary\":\"The term identifies the Messiah as faithfully bearing the Father’s light, while the moon serves as a heavenly symbol or inscription of that witness.\",\"consensus_core_identification\":\"Jesus Christ, the Messiah, as God’s faithful witness.\",\"book_core_identification\":\"The Messiah as the faithful bearer of the Father’s light and witness.\",\"core_relation\":\"EQUIVALENT\",\"extension_relation\":\"SAME_CASES\",\"boundary_disagreement\":\"No material boundary disagreement is evident. The book adds a lunar-symbolic interpretation but does not identify the moon instead of the Messiah or exclude the central Christological case.\",\"material_contradiction\":\"None. The book’s identification of the Messiah agrees with the settled Christological identification; its moon imagery is a supplementary typological gloss.\",\"rationale\":\"Reduced to their minimum cores, both readings identify the Faithful Witness as Jesus/the Messiah. The book’s language about bearing the Father’s light through the night and the moon writing this witness in the sky adds imagery drawn from Psalm 89:37, but it neither replaces the Christological referent nor materially changes the cases covered.\",\"citation_support\":\"PARTIAL\",\"strongest_objection\":\"Although Revelation 1:5 and 3:14 directly support the Messianic identification and Psalm 89:37 supplies the moon/witness connection, the specific claim that the Messiah bears the Father’s light through the night is a typological synthesis rather than something explicitly stated by the printed verses.\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}"
        }
